1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,296
Hi! This is InspectAgent, my solo entry for the OpenCV AI Competition 2026, powered by AWS.

2
00:00:09,646 --> 00:00:16,052
It is an agentic visual inspection system. OpenCV 5 looks at every part, and what it sees

3
00:00:16,052 --> 00:00:18,499
decides what the system does next.

4
00:00:19,849 --> 00:00:26,317
Most automated inspection is one-shot. Take one photo, run one fixed pipeline, and output pass

5
00:00:26,317 --> 00:00:26,868
or fail.

6
00:00:27,218 --> 00:00:33,211
But on a real production line, the photo itself is often the problem: blur, poor exposure,

7
00:00:33,211 --> 00:00:34,876
glare, or a rotated part.

8
00:00:35,226 --> 00:00:41,599
In my tests, a one-shot OpenCV pipeline was right only half the time when the image was blurry

9
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:42,480
or had glare.

10
00:00:43,830 --> 00:00:45,963
A human inspector behaves differently.

11
00:00:46,313 --> 00:00:51,592
When the view is bad, they fix the light and look again. When something looks odd, they lean in

12
00:00:51,592 --> 00:00:55,593
for a closer look. And before stopping the line, they call a supervisor.

13
00:00:55,943 --> 00:00:58,333
InspectAgent does the same three things.

14
00:00:59,683 --> 00:01:05,059
Here is the architecture. A judge or operator opens a Lambda function URL.

15
00:01:05,409 --> 00:01:13,561
The Lambda runs as an arm64 container on AWS Graviton, with FastAPI, the agent executor, and

16
00:01:13,561 --> 00:01:15,244
the OpenCV 5 tools.

17
00:01:15,594 --> 00:01:18,452
Amazon Bedrock hosts Claude as an optional planner.

18
00:01:18,802 --> 00:01:26,029
Traces and evidence images go to S3, approval requests go to DynamoDB, and SNS emails the

19
00:01:26,029 --> 00:01:26,760
approver.

20
00:01:27,110 --> 00:01:32,934
CloudWatch and X-Ray provide observability, and everything is deployed with one SAM template.

21
00:01:34,284 --> 00:01:37,505
The agent runs a perception, decision, and action loop.

22
00:01:37,855 --> 00:01:44,756
Perception is pure OpenCV 5: a quality gate using the variance of the Laplacian, exposure and

23
00:01:44,756 --> 00:01:51,805
glare. Registration with ORB, RANSAC and ECC. A misalignment-tolerant residual against a golden

24
00:01:51,805 --> 00:01:54,773
reference. And a close-up re-inspection.

25
00:01:55,123 --> 00:02:00,205
The planner picks the next tool, and guardrails in the executor make sure no part passes

26
00:02:00,205 --> 00:02:04,189
without a real check, and no critical action happens without a human.

27
00:02:05,539 --> 00:02:08,547
Let's see it work. This part was captured out of focus.

28
00:02:08,897 --> 00:02:14,986
The quality gate measures a sharpness of 84, below the threshold of 120, so the agent

29
00:02:14,986 --> 00:02:16,705
recaptures with refocus.

30
00:02:17,055 --> 00:02:24,177
Sharpness jumps to about 260. Now it aligns the part, runs defect detection, finds nothing, and

31
00:02:24,177 --> 00:02:24,927
passes it.

32
00:02:26,277 --> 00:02:29,413
This one is harder: glare, and a very faint defect.

33
00:02:29,763 --> 00:02:36,184
OpenCV finds three and a half percent saturated pixels, so the agent enables the polarizer.

34
00:02:36,534 --> 00:02:41,694
At the production threshold, no defect is found. But the peak residual is close to the

35
00:02:41,694 --> 00:02:42,294
threshold.

36
00:02:42,644 --> 00:02:48,370
So instead of passing, the agent takes a close-up at double sensitivity, finds the spot, and

37
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:49,428
rejects the part.

38
00:02:50,778 --> 00:02:53,893
Finally, a blurry capture of a part with a missing hole.

39
00:02:54,243 --> 00:02:57,571
After refocusing, detection finds a critical defect.

40
00:02:57,921 --> 00:03:04,587
Quarantining a production lot is a big decision, so the agent does not act alone. It requests

41
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:10,465
human approval. On AWS, the request is stored in DynamoDB and emailed through SNS.

42
00:03:10,815 --> 00:03:15,316
The reviewer approves it, and the decision is recorded with the reviewer and the time.

43
00:03:16,666 --> 00:03:23,150
I evaluated InspectAgent on 200 reproducible parts with ground-truth defects and capture

44
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:23,813
problems.

45
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:32,066
Compared with the same OpenCV detector run only once, pass or fail accuracy rose from 72 to 99

46
00:03:32,066 --> 00:03:32,739
percent.

47
00:03:33,089 --> 00:03:39,057
Defective parts slipping through dropped from 16 to 2 percent, and good parts wrongly rejected

48
00:03:39,057 --> 00:03:41,089
dropped from 40 percent to zero.

49
00:03:41,439 --> 00:03:43,487
The biggest gains are on blur and glare.

50
00:03:44,837 --> 00:03:47,674
Failure handling and human control are built in.

51
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:54,140
If a planner, even an LLM, tries to skip the human on a critical defect, the executor blocks

52
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:54,339
it.

53
00:03:54,689 --> 00:03:59,126
If Bedrock is unavailable, the loop falls back to the rule planner and logs it.

54
00:03:59,476 --> 00:04:04,724
And every step is logged with its OpenCV measurements, so each decision can be audited.

55
00:04:06,074 --> 00:04:11,908
There are limits. These results come from a synthetic inspection cell. Real parts are harder,

56
00:04:11,908 --> 00:04:14,543
and real-image evaluation is my next step.

57
00:04:14,893 --> 00:04:19,309
Two faint spots were still missed, and four stains were labeled as minor spots.

58
00:04:19,659 --> 00:04:23,990
The golden-reference method also needs one reference image per part type.

59
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:30,956
InspectAgent shows that the biggest gain does not come from a better detector, but from letting

60
00:04:30,956 --> 00:04:34,385
the system notice a bad observation and take a better one.

61
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:40,303
The code, the technical report, and the evaluation are on GitHub. Thank you for watching!
